    sections: [
      {
        type: 'about',
        tabLabel: 'About',
        pages: [
          {
            title: 'Biritch',
            subtitle: 'The Original Bridge (1886)',
            content: [
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: "Biritch, or Russian Whist, is the earliest known form of Bridge â€” the game that the word 'Bridge' originally referred to before Auction Bridge and Contract Bridge evolved from it. It is a 4-player partnership trick-taking game using a standard 52-card deck. Unlike Whist, no trump is turned up; instead the dealer (or dealer's partner) declares trumps or calls 'biritch' (no-trump). The dealer's partner's hand is then exposed as a 'Dummy', played by the dealer â€” the defining innovation that would eventually lead to modern Contract Bridge.",
              },
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: "The game was played in the Russian colony at Constantinople (Istanbul) during the 1880s. John Collinson, a British railway engineer working in Turkey between 1880â€“1884, learned the game there and introduced it to friends in England. He published a small booklet titled 'Biritch, or Russian Whist' on 9 July 1886, registered at Stationers' Hall â€” the earliest known document describing any form of Bridge.",
              },
            ],
          },
          {
            title: 'Setup',
            subtitle: 'Table, deck, equipment, and deal',
            content: [
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: '4 players in fixed partnerships, seated opposite each other. Cutting for partners, shuffling, and dealing are the same as at Short Whist.',
              },
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: 'Standard 52-card deck. Card ranking as at Whist: A (high), K, Q, J, 10, 9, 8, 7, 6, 5, 4, 3, 2.',
              },
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: 'Standard 52-card deck. Score sheet.',
              },
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: '13 cards dealt to each player. Unlike Whist, NO card is turned up for trumps.',
              },
            ],
          },
          {
            title: 'Variations',
            subtitle: 'Known ways this game changes',
            content: [
              {
                type: 'list',
                style: 'unordered',
                items: [
                  {
                    text: 'Auction Bridge (Successor): Evolved from Biritch in the 1900s. All four players bid competitively for the right to name trumps, replacing fixed dealer declaration.',
                  },
                  {
                    text: 'Contract Bridge (Modern Successor): Developed by Harold Vanderbilt in 1925. Only tricks bid and won count toward game; overtricks score separately. The dominant form of Bridge today.',
                  },
                ],
              },
            ],
          },
        ],
      },
    ],
    description: "Biritch, or Russian Whist, is the earliest known form of Bridge â€” the game that the word 'Bridge' originally referred to before Auction Bridge and Contract Bridge evolved from it. It is a 4-player partnership trick-taking game using a standard 52-card deck. Unlike Whist, no trump is turned up; instead the dealer (or dealer's partner) declares trumps or calls 'biritch' (no-trump). The dealer's partner's hand is then exposed as a 'Dummy', played by the dealer â€” the defining innovation that would eventually lead to modern Contract Bridge.",
    tags: [
