    historyContent: {
      origins: "The game was played in the Russian colony at Constantinople (Istanbul) during the 1880s. John Collinson, a British railway engineer working in Turkey between 1880â€“1884, learned the game there and introduced it to friends in England. He published a small booklet titled 'Biritch, or Russian Whist' on 9 July 1886, registered at Stationers' Hall â€” the earliest known document describing any form of Bridge.",
      originCountries: [
        'Turkey',
        'Russia',
        'United Kingdom',
      ],
      timeline: [
        '1880-1884: John Collinson plays Biritch in the Russian colony at Constantinople.',
        "1886: Collinson publishes 'Biritch, or Russian Whist' â€” the first bridge rules ever printed.",
        "1906: Collinson writes to The Saturday Review confirming the game's origins.",
        '1890s-1920s: Evolves into Auction Bridge (players bid for the right to name trumps).',
        '1925: Harold Vanderbilt develops Contract Bridge from Auction Bridge.',
        '1940-41: One of the four known surviving copies is destroyed in the London Blitz.',
      ],
      evolution: "Biritch introduced two revolutionary concepts to the Whist family: (1) the dealer declares trumps rather than turning a card, and (2) the partner's hand is exposed as 'Dummy.' These innovations laid the foundation for all subsequent Bridge variants. Only three copies of the original 1886 booklet survive (Bodleian Library Oxford, Cambridge University Library, Public Record Office London).",
      cultural: "Biritch represents the missing link between Whist (no trump declaration, no dummy) and modern Bridge. The word 'bridge' likely derives from 'biritch', though the etymology remains debated by scholars. The game's transmission from the Russian community in Constantinople via a British engineer embodies the cosmopolitan card-game culture of the 19th-century Ottoman Empire.",
    },
    setupContent: {
      players: '4 players in fixed partnerships, seated opposite each other. Cutting for partners, shuffling, and dealing are the same as at Short Whist.',
